From f05713e245d2cc93ce96cb7ce0a26f6ca960995b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vihan Aggarwal <mooimacow1234567@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 17:03:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Fork the canonical-mask builder before CUDA init instead
 of at the clock's start

Record #350 forks rank 0 at t0, i.e. forks the whole warmed-up trainer: fork() copies its page
tables, and every pre-fork page the trainer writes in the first steps takes a copy-on-write
fault on rank 0's launch path, which every rank then waits for at its next collective. The
first 25 steps of the 17 ANVIL2 logs carry 130-600 ms of startup overhead (sd 122 ms, ~all of
the run-to-run wall variance).

The child is now forked first thing in train_gpt.py, before CUDA is initialized, and blocks on
a pipe; start() releases it at the clock's start, so the build itself still runs entirely on
the clock. A child whose trainer exits before the clock sees EOF and exits; a child that died
while waiting makes start() a no-op and wait() falls back to the inline build, as before.

Systems-only: the mask is byte-identical (tools/tests/test_canonical_mask_prefork.py).

